ned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f65d032-9acc-5fe6-b7c2-b8293576cb4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b58f5b7-691b-5929-b3f3-155b7b9c7877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535364} {:answer/id #uuid "a20414db-0f55-57cd-8794-164dd664c073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535385} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd45c59-1df7-5e4b-acf6-01971d846600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535391} {:answer/id #uuid "c778cb8e-20a2-54c1-b08c-f697e2fd2f8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535392} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf3171e-b700-5117-8881-f76c1e516256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddf3171e-b700-5117-8881-f76c1e516256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535398} :answer-field/id #uuid "77fb4fb9-d14b-5e45-b0a7-fd3b48f931e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "acfdff43-d0c5-5a54-b1b4-680d286758da" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2\\sqrt{3},{120}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f65d032-9acc-5fe6-b7c2-b8293576cb4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ba955d-0687-5313-8a94-1e8d43146764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535363} {:answer/id #uuid "5383f70f-b2db-58bf-be9a-991190a79f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535368} {:answer/id #uuid "95b14a76-aada-5d75-9c60-9bc381e42b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535382} {:answer/id #uuid "b4221dca-faf4-5364-b247-a200dbba83c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535388} {:answer/id #uuid "ebad5aa5-4ceb-50fd-960a-5ea2aeef6720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :